# The variant registry. Every script on `main` reads this and nothing else to decide what
# "all variants" means, so adding or retiring a variant is a one-line edit here.
#
#   <variant>  <branch>  <family>  <capabilities>
#
# `capabilities` is a comma-separated list, or `-` for none. It records workload knobs the
# branch actually implements SERVER-side, which is not the same as knobs k6 will send: k6 is
# byte-identical everywhere and sends them all, and Spring ignores unknown properties, so an
# unimplemented knob is silently discarded rather than rejected. run-suite.sh reads this to
# warn when a knob is set for variants that cannot honour it.
#
#   reserve-delay   RESERVE_DELAY_MS — `reserveDelayMs` on CreateItemRequest plus a sleep on
#                   the reserve path, paid only once the reserve is known to succeed and with
#                   the row/aggregate lock held. Every branch here, since 2026-08-06. TO
#                   needs a column (V5__reserve_delay.sql on TO-1/TO-2/TO-4; V2 on TO-3 and
#                   on TO-3-pessimistic, which forked from it); ES
#                   needs none — the value rides InventoryCreatedEvent and is replayed into
#                   the aggregate, with the sleep in the @CommandHandler so a replay or
#                   snapshot load does not pay it.
#
#   payload-bytes   PAYLOAD_BYTES as a real per-reserve cost. Every branch here, since
#                   2026-08-06. ES needs no schema change — the padding rides
#                   InventoryCreatedEvent and is replayed into the aggregate. On TO it has to
#                   be a column on inventory_state, which only TO-3 had; TO-1/TO-2/TO-4
#                   gained it as V6__additional_bytes.sql. Before that the field was accepted
#                   and carried on the creation event, but never reached the row each reserve
#                   rewrites, so the copy-on-write cost the lever measures never happened.
#
# `variant` is the identity used everywhere downstream: it names the image tag
# (inventory-reservation-<variant>:latest, lowercased), the worktree directory, and the
# bench-results/<variant>_<scenario>_<ts>/ run directory. There is no bench.env any more:
# run-suite.sh exports VARIANT from this column and bench.sh builds RUN_NAME from it, so the
# name here is the only name a run is ever filed under. It need not equal the branch name —
# the two columns are separate for exactly that reason — but keeping them equal is what makes
# `--only <name>` guessable.
#
# `TO-3-pessimistic` was RETIRED FROM THE REGISTRY on 2026-08-17 and is a branch only. It is not a
# fifth TO design; it is TO-3 with the reserve path changed from optimistic `@Version` retry to
# `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` (6 files, all under src/), and it forked from the PRE-merge TO-3.
#
# It was pulled because the pair it existed for stopped being a pair. TO-3 has since absorbed the
# retry rebuild and the pool split (below) and TO-3-pessimistic did not, so
# `--only TO-3,TO-3-pessimistic` varied the lock, the retry mechanism, the pool topology and the
# thread widths at once — four dimensions, presented as a locking A/B. Restoring it to the suite
# means porting the TO-3 merge to it first; re-add a row here when that is done.
#
# `TO-2-opt` is the same kind of entry on TO-2: the outbox DELIVERY TRIGGER changed, nothing
# else. TO-2 submits one executor task per NOTIFY, an open loop with no backpressure, so a
# commit burst becomes a delivery burst that drives the order-worker pool into row contention.
# TO-2-opt discards the notification payload and treats NOTIFY as a wake-up only: a burst
# coalesces into one drain pass that delivers in bounded pages, blocking on each page before
# fetching the next — TO-1's closed-loop `drain()` contract with the scheduler tick replaced by
# a signal. EVENT_DELIVERY_THREADS, ORDER_WORKER_THREADS, the V2 trigger and the schema are
# unchanged, so the pair isolates trigger SHAPE. Run as `--only TO-2,TO-2-opt`.
#
# It exists because the W-base breakpoint pair put TO-2 at db_write p95 404 ms / conflict_ratio
# 4.64 / 41% rejected and an undrained 439k backlog, against TO-1's 0.96 ms / 0.37 / 0.25%. TO-1
# reaches that only by lagging delivery ten minutes (publish_lag p95 pegged at the 600 s clamp),
# i.e. accidental admission control. The question TO-2-opt answers is whether backpressure alone
# buys TO-1's write profile at TO-2's delivery latency — so read `publish_lag_p95` alongside the
# contention signals, not instead of them.
#
# One knob is TO-2-opt's alone: EVENT_DELIVERY_BATCH_SIZE (default 1000), publications per drain
# page. It is not in the capabilities column because it is not a workload knob — k6 never sends
# it and no point in points.env sets it.
#
# `TO-2-newT` is the third entry of this kind on TO-2, and the only one that changes the
# TRANSACTION SHAPE. Delivery trigger, schema, pools, retry policy and metric names are TO-2's;
# what moves is the boundary. TO-2 reserves an order in one transaction — a command per line, each
# joining it, each doing SELECT -> reserve() -> versioned UPDATE -> INSERT reservations -> INSERT
# event_publication — so line 1's exclusive row lock is held across every later line's read, its
# reserveDelayMs sleep and its outbox write: 3N+4 round trips, N of them under the lock.
#
# TO-2-newT splits that into read (one findAllById for the whole order), modify (reserve() per line
# against an in-memory working copy) and write. Only the write is transactional, and it issues the
# versioned batch UPDATE of inventory_state LAST, after the outbox rows, the reservations and the
# order — so the exclusive locks exist for one statement instead of for the whole order. Run as
# `--only TO-2,TO-2-newT`.
#
# TO-2-newT ALSO JITTERS THE RETRY BACKOFF (2026-08-18), so this pair is not single-variable
# either. No other TO branch does. `OrderRetryPolicy.baseDelayMsFor` is still the shared
# 25/50/100/200 ms curve; `delayMsFor` spreads each delay uniformly over `[0.5 x base, 1.5 x base)`.
# `--only TO-2,TO-2-newT` therefore varies transaction shape AND retry correlation together, and a
# conflict or throughput difference between them cannot be attributed to the boundary alone.
# Isolating either needs a third branch that changes only the other.
#
# It is there because this branch RAISES the conflict rate by construction, and deterministic
# backoff turns that into a convoy: orders that collide at T all wake at T+25, collide again, all
# wake at T+75. Cheap when conflicts are rare, self-inflicted when they are not.
#
# Symmetric on purpose, not AWS's full `rand(0, base)`: the expected delay stays exactly the base,
# so an exhausted order still spends 375 ms of accumulated backoff and `order_retry_backoff_time`
# stays comparable across every TO branch. Full jitter would have halved it and made that series
# measure a different thing here than on its pair. Confirmed on a local run — 56 exhausted orders
# spent 20.564 s against a 21.0 s nominal budget.
#
# TWO THINGS RIDE THE SAME CHANGE, and a result that names only one of them is wrong.
#
# 1. Reads are no longer serialised by row locks, so conflicts and retries go UP while lock hold
#    time goes down. That trade IS the result. Read `conflict_ratio` and the rejected share next to
#    `db_write` latency, never one without the other.
#
# 2. The reserveDelayMs sleep now holds neither a row lock NOR a Hikari connection, where on TO-2 it
#    holds both. Against 50 connections and 200 worker threads that is a second, distinct effect. A
#    RESERVE_DELAY_MS=0 pair isolates effect 1; only a nonzero pair shows both, and it cannot tell
#    them apart on its own.
#
# METRIC GRANULARITY DIFFERS, and the dashboards cannot see it. `state_load_time` and
# `state_persist_time` are one sample per ORDER here and one per LINE on TO-2, because there is now
# one load and one write per order. The names and tags are identical, so every panel resolves and
# every panel comparing the two silently compares different quantities — divide TO-2's by
# ITEMS_PER_ORDER, or compare the *_count series to see the ratio directly.
#
# `TO-3` ABSORBED THE FORMER TO-3-mod AND TO-3-mod-A (merged 2026-08-17, PR #4). Those were
# separate registry rows until then; they are gone, and TO-3 now carries both changes. Any run
# directory named `TO-3` from BEFORE that merge measured the old branch — different retry mechanism,
# one flat pool, 200/200 threads — so do not put pre- and post-merge TO-3 rows in one table.
#
# Two things came in with it, on top of the outbox design TO-3 always had.
#
# 1. THE CONFLICT RETRY MECHANISM, rebuilt to match the ES branches'. Retry POLICY is unchanged
#    (5 attempts, 25/50/100/200 ms, same counters), so nothing measured can be blamed on a different
#    attempt budget or backoff curve. The old path waited inside Spring's `@Retryable` interceptor,
#    which sleeps on the `order-worker` thread that is retrying — up to 375 ms of a worker doing
#    nothing, and under load the pool could sit entirely parked in backoff while orders queued
#    behind it. It now schedules instead of sleeping and runs the retried attempt on a pool of its
#    own, which is how ES has always worked (Axon's `RetryingCallback` re-dispatches inline onto its
#    retry pool). Both properties used to be confounds bundled into every TO-vs-ES number.
#
#    Which topology a run uses is a setting, and a run must record it:
#      ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=true   (default) retries run on their own pool, ES-shaped.
#      ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=false  retries hand back to the worker pool.
#    They are not merely different widths: on the hand-off path a retry rejoins a FIFO queue at the
#    TAIL and waits behind orders admitted after it, so its real wait is backoff + queue depth.
#
#    Expect a HIGHER exhaustion rate than the pre-merge branch in either topology — work that no
#    longer blocks admits more concurrent attempts, hence more conflicts.
#
# 2. THE LANE WIDTHS: worker 200 -> 150, retry 12 -> 50, http 200 -> 99.
#
#    These arrived with a lane-routed TWO-POOL DataSource (app-pool for HTTP reads, write-pool for
#    everything else), which was REMOVED again on 2026-08-17. The widths stayed; the split did not.
#    TO is back to one flat Hikari pool on every branch, sized by DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS in .env (350).
#    The split lived on this branch from 2026-08-15 to 2026-08-17 — git history has it if the
#    read-isolation argument is ever wanted back. Nothing reads DB_APP_POOL_SIZE or
#    DB_WRITE_POOL_SIZE any more and compose no longer forwards them.
#
# It publishes four series the pre-rebuild branches do not — `order_retry_backoff_time` (accumulated
# wait per retried order), `order_retry_rejected_total`, and `order_retry_pool_queued`. TO-2-opt and
# TO-3-pessimistic still carry the blocking `@Retryable` and publish none of them, so panels using
# them read empty there.
#
# 3. THE RETRY POOL IS GONE AGAIN, ON ALL FOUR (2026-08-18). TO-3-oneExec proved the topology and
#    TO-1, TO-2, TO-3 and TO-4 all took it, so `app.order-retry` no longer exists on any of them.
#
#    One ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor of 200 threads, all named `order-worker-*`, runs first attempts
#    AND retried attempts: the worker thread that failed the attempt schedules the retry on its own
#    pool and returns, the backoff is served in that pool's DelayedWorkQueue (no thread, no
#    connection), and when it comes due an `order-worker` thread runs it.
#
#    200 = the old 150 worker + 50 retry, so executing width and connection demand are unchanged and
#    the change is retry TOPOLOGY only. NOTHING IN-TREE STILL RUNS THE TWO-POOL TOPOLOGY, so the A/B
#    is against runs taken before 2026-08-18, not against a sibling variant. `TO-3-oneExec` was that
#    sibling and is RETIRED from this registry — it is now the same code as TO-3.
#
#    EVERY ONE OF THE FOUR MUST BE RUN AT ORDER_WORKER_THREADS=200. compose defaults that variable
#    to 150 for every TO branch, which silently makes them 50 execution threads narrower than every
#    run taken before the merge — a resourcing difference presented as a topology result, with
#    nothing failing to show for it. `variants.d/TO-1.env`, `TO-2.env`, `TO-3.env` and `TO-4.env`
#    export the 200 and run-suite.sh sources them per variant; each branch also logs its width and
#    this remedy at startup. Exporting 200 globally is now SAFE for the four (it was not while TO-3
#    ran 150 + 50 against a 300-connection write pool) but would also move TO-2-opt and
#    TO-3-pessimistic from 150 to 200, changing their measured config again — hence the per-variant
#    files rather than a new compose default.
#
#    Do not read the merge as a third retry setting alongside ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL. The
#    queue POSITION a retry lands in is the same as the =false hand-off — a DelayedWorkQueue orders
#    by due time and a first attempt is a zero-delay task, so a retry sorts behind everything
#    submitted before it came due and ahead of everything after, exactly where a FIFO tail puts it.
#    What went away is the second POOL and the cross-pool hand-off, so the result is about pool count
#    and lane isolation, not about queue discipline. Both ORDER_RETRY_* knobs are inert on all four
#    and they say so in their startup logs.
#
#    METRICS CHANGED WITH IT. `order_retry_pool_active` is GONE (no separate pool can be active; a
#    panel target for it now returns nothing on TO-1..TO-4, which is correct rather than broken).
#    `order_retry_pool_queued` IS still published but means something narrower — retries scheduled
#    and not yet started — because the merged pool's `executor_queued_tasks` holds ready first
#    attempts and backing-off retries together. Ready backlog is the difference of the two.
#    `executor_*{name="orderWorkerExecutor"}` is bound by hand, since the pool is not a
#    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor and Boot only auto-binds those. Two of those series read differently
#    now, both because a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor is not a ThreadPoolTaskExecutor:
#    `executor_pool_max_threads` is 2147483647 and NOT the width (an STPE grows only to its core size
#    and leaves max at Integer.MAX_VALUE) — read `executor_pool_core_threads`, which is 200; and
#    `executor_queued_tasks` includes retries still in backoff.
#
# ALL FOUR NOW SHARE ONE FLAT POOL of DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=350, and all four fit it with headroom:
#
#   TO-1  99 http + 200 worker + 10 outbox poller  + 1 republisher = 310
#   TO-2  99 http + 200 worker + 20 notify workers + 1 republisher = 320
#   TO-3  99 http + 200 worker +                     1 republisher = 300
#   TO-4  99 http + 200 worker +                     1 republisher = 300
#
# The worker term is 200 because the retry lane merged into it (see 3 above); the sums are the same
# as when it was 150 worker + 50 retry, which is what makes runs across the merge comparable — but
# only if ORDER_WORKER_THREADS=200 actually reaches the container.
#
# One connection per demander — every TO handler is Propagation.REQUIRED, where a busy ES thread
# holds two. The pool was 300 until 2026-08-17, which left TO-1 and TO-2 oversubscribed; nothing
# warns when a flat pool runs short, so the symptom is Hikari wait time.
#
# TO-1 differs from the others in where @Async work runs, and it is PRE-EXISTING rather than a
# consequence of the port: it has TWO TaskExecutor beans (orderWorkerExecutor + outboxPollerExecutor),
# so @Async cannot resolve a unique one, finds no bean named `taskExecutor`, and falls back to
# SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor — a new thread per invocation. On TO-2/TO-3/TO-4 the same
# @ApplicationModuleListener runs on the order-worker pool, where event DELIVERY competes with
# reservation EXECUTION. Do not read a TO-1 vs TO-2/TO-4 delivery difference as a design property
# without accounting for it.
#
# NOT PORTED: TO-2-opt and TO-3-pessimistic. Both still carry the blocking `@Retryable`, one flat
# pool and their own widths, so:
#   `--only TO-3,TO-3-pessimistic` is no longer a lock A/B — it now varies the lock, the retry
#       mechanism, the pool topology and the widths at once. TO-3-pessimistic was retired from the
#       registry over exactly this, so that pair cannot be run by the suite at all until it is
#       ported and re-added.
#   `--only TO-2,TO-2-opt` is no longer a delivery-trigger A/B — TO-2 has the retry rebuild and
#       TO-2-opt does not. TO-2-opt IS still in the registry, so this one runs and silently
#       compares two different retry mechanisms. Port it, or say so in every run note.
# Note both still read ORDER_WORKER_THREADS and HTTP_THREADS, so compose moves them to 150/99
# regardless of the 200/200 in their own yaml.
#
# The knobs below are forwarded by docker-compose with defaults MATCHING TO-3's shipped values, so
# an unset run reproduces TO-3 exactly and the other TO branches inherit the same widths (they read
# ORDER_WORKER_THREADS and HTTP_THREADS; ORDER_RETRY_* is inert on them, having no
# app.order-retry). Keeping them in step with TO-3's application.yaml is manual — a
# compose default always sets the variable and therefore OVERRIDES the branch, which is how a
# 150-worker branch would otherwise quietly run at 200.
#   ORDER_WORKER_THREADS  (150) — a blocking backoff rarely starves a pool this wide, so a
#       retry-mechanism A/B is expected to show little at 150 and to diverge as it falls
#       (64, 32, 16). A null result at 150 is the predicted result, not a failed run.
#   ORDER_RETRY_THREADS   (50) — equal to ORDER_WORKER_THREADS gives "separate lane, equally wide";
#       lower reproduces a narrow waist.
#   ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL (true) — above.
#   HTTP_THREADS (99) — see the pool section.
#
# THE POOL SPLIT, AND WHY IT IS GONE (removed 2026-08-17). TO-3 briefly ran two Hikari pools behind
# a lane-routed DataSource — `app-pool` for HTTP reads, `write-pool` for the accept transaction,
# order workers, retry pool and republisher — mirroring ES's spring.datasource + axonDataSource
# split. The argument for it stands and is worth restating, because it is the argument for reading
# TO's pool numbers carefully rather than for the code: ES's write path CANNOT starve ES's HTTP
# reads, TO's can. That is the mechanism behind the TO-2 collapse (Hikari pegged at 300 active /
# ~119 pending, the accept path sharing the pool, throughput halved), and comparing the families on
# pool pressure measures that topology difference rather than a persistence model.
#
# It was removed anyway, and two facts made the isolation cheap to give up. First, k6's read
# scenario is gated on READ_RATE, which defaults to 0 — so on a standard run NOTHING calls the three
# GET endpoints and app-pool sat idle, holding 50 connections open (Hikari's minimumIdle defaults to
# maximumPoolSize) to protect traffic that was not there. Second, keeping it meant TO-3 alone had a
# DataSource topology no other TO branch had, on top of the retry rebuild it already carried.
#
# What replaced it: ONE flat pool on every TO branch, DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=350 from .env, covering
# http + worker + retry + delivery + republisher with headroom on all four. Nothing recomputes that
# sum at startup any more and nothing logs `[POOLS]`, so an over-wide sweep shows up as Hikari wait
# time rather than a warning — raising HTTP_THREADS or ORDER_WORKER_THREADS is still a pool change
# even though it does not look like one.
#
# If the isolation is ever wanted back, git history has it: the branch carried it from 2026-08-15
# (5a163df) to 2026-08-17, and the read side only means something under a run with READ_RATE set.
#
# Unlike ES, TO holds ONE connection per busy thread: every handler is Propagation.REQUIRED, so
# there is no second, non-transactional connection like the one Axon's DataSourceConnectionProvider
# takes per event-store call. That 2:1 is a property of the persistence model, not a setting.
#
# THE ENVELOPES (2026-08-17). Both families SPEND their write pool: each one's write-lane threads
# are sized so their peak demand equals the pool that serves them. Because TO holds one connection
# per busy thread and ES two, they buy
#
#   TO-3              300 demanders:  99 http + 150 worker + 50 retry + 1 republisher
#                     in one flat pool of 350 per node
#   ES-4-NullLock     175 write-lane threads:  82 command + 30 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections
#                                              (+ 99 http, NOT in the sum — see its entry)
#                     + 50 hikari = 400 per node
#
# That ~2:1 connection cost per thread is a property of the persistence model — a RESULT, not a
# setting. ES-1-NullLock and ES-2-NullLock carry the same widths, so any of the three serves as the
# ES side. (This table named ES-4-NullLock-A until 2026-08-17, with a `12 http + 91 command` shape
# that branch never actually implemented; it is retired from the registry now.)
#
# THE TWO ENVELOPES NO LONGER MATCH: 350 against 400, where 2026-08-16 had them equal at 400. TO's
# write pool went 350 -> 300 with its worker lane (200 -> 150) and ES was deliberately left alone.
# So the families present Postgres with different footprints again — the very confound the equal
# envelope was introduced to remove. It is accepted, not overlooked; any cross-family table built
# from runs after this date must say so, and runs from before it are not config-identical to runs
# after, so do not mix them in one table.
#
# THE LANE WIDTHS ARE NOT A CROSS-FAMILY RATIO. An earlier version mirrored TO's admission : worker
# : retry shape onto ES; that was wrong, because there is no thread-for-thread correspondence
# between the families. TO carries a whole order on ONE thread for ~6-11 ms. On ES an order costs N+2 saga
# activations at ~6.7 ms and N+1 command executions at ~3 ms, so by Little's law the saga lane needs
# ~2.4x the command lane — where the mirrored shape gave it a fifth.
#
# Measured on ES-4-NullLock-A_capacity_W-base_20260815T212646Z: saga pool 12/12 active with 48
# queued for the entire 21-minute load phase, command pool ~7 busy of 91, axon-jdbc-pool 30-39
# active of 350, order e2e p95 111 s. The saga lane was the constraint and 90% of the connection
# budget went unused. SAGA_WORKER_THREADS therefore goes 12 -> 64, total-segments 60 -> 64 to match
# (a segment is processed sequentially, so it is the concurrency ceiling), and Tomcat takes the
# remainder at 12 — the accept path is one aggregate append against an order costing N+1 of them.
#
# WriteLaneCoverageTest and AxonPoolCoverageTest each assert their own half of the budget.
#
# Read `hikaricp_connections_active` per pool, plus `order_retry_pool_active` (TO) and
# `saga_pool_active{pool="retry"}` (ES): a run that pinned either retry pool at its ceiling measured
# pool width, not the persistence model, and should be re-run wider. Both families also leave
# Tomcat's accept-count at Boot's default 100, so offered load beyond the HTTP thread cap is
# REFUSED past that backlog — check the 5xx rate before reading a run as merely slow.
#
# `ES-4-NullLock` is the same kind of entry on the ES side, and the mirror image of that one:
# ES-4 with the aggregate lock REMOVED (`NullLockFactory` instead of `PessimisticLockFactory`
# on the InventoryItem repository), so concurrent commands on one aggregate race for the same
# sequence number and are resolved by the event store's unique constraint plus the gateway
# retry instead of by in-JVM serialisation. The copy-on-write cache, the cache-fed snapshotter and
# the saga are byte-identical to ES-4. Run it as a pair: `--only ES-4,ES-4-NullLock`, ideally at
# DISTINCT_ITEMS=1, which is where a lock A/B is visible at all.
#
# THE PAIR IS NO LONGER SINGLE-VARIABLE (2026-08-17). Two thread widths moved here and not on ES-4:
# Tomcat 200 -> 99 (`HTTP_THREADS`, matching TO-3's admission width) and the command pool
# 64 -> 82. 82 is not a width chosen on its own merits — it is the residue of the connection budget
# once retry 30, saga 60 and the 3 projections are paid for, so the branch consumes the 350 that
# docker-compose passes EXACTLY: 2 x (82 + 30 + 60 + 3) = 350. To read `--only ES-4,ES-4-NullLock`
# as the lock alone, port both widths to ES-4 first, or say in the run notes that they differ.
#
# The 99 Tomcat threads are deliberately OUTSIDE that budget and are a known gap, not an oversight:
# createOrderReservation calls sendAndWait on the Tomcat thread and SimpleCommandBus handles it
# there, so an in-flight POST holds two axon-jdbc-pool connections like any command thread. True
# peak demand is 2 x (99 + 175) = 548 against a pool of 350; it holds only because offered load,
# not the thread cap, bounds POSTs in flight. Capping Tomcat low and deriving the pool from the full
# sum would close it; NO BRANCH ACTUALLY DOES THIS — the ES-4-NullLock-A notes claim it, but that
# branch has no `server.tomcat` block in code. Watch
# `hikaricp_connections_timeout_total{pool="axon-jdbc-pool"}` — a starved command stalls 5s and
# then fails TERMINALLY, showing up as latency and rejections rather than as an error.
#
# `ES-4-NullLock-oneExec` is ES-4-NullLock with the retry pool demoted to a TIMER: the retried
# command is handed back to `sagaCommandExecutor` instead of executing on the retry pool, and that
# pool absorbs the 30 threads the retry lane no longer executes on (command 82 -> 112). It is the ES
# analogue of TO's ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=false.
#
# It exists because the two-lane shape is not a design choice on the other ES branches — it is a
# consequence nobody opted into. Axon's `RetryingCallback.RetryDispatch.run()` calls
# `commandBus.dispatch()` INLINE and the autoconfigured SimpleCommandBus handles on the calling
# thread, so the whole retried command executes wherever the scheduled task runs. Axon documents
# neither that consequence nor any sizing guidance for the retry executor; it follows from two
# documented properties (SimpleCommandBus runs on the dispatching thread; RetryScheduler schedules
# a task on its executor) plus verification against the 4.11.2 bytecode. Treat it as version-
# specific behaviour rather than a guarantee.
#
# THE PAIR IS SINGLE-VARIABLE BY CONSTRUCTION, which is rare here and the reason to trust it:
#
#   ES-4-NullLock          2 x ( 82 command + 30 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) = 350
#   ES-4-NullLock-oneExec  2 x (112 command +  0 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) = 350
#
# Equal executing threads (175), equal Axon connections (350, the AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE default),
# identical retry policy (5 attempts, 25/50/100/200 ms), identical Tomcat cap (99, outside both
# sums). The retry lane drops out because a thread that only calls execute(...) opens no
# transaction and takes no connection, and the backoff is served in the DelayedWorkQueue on no
# thread at all. Run as `--only ES-4-NullLock,ES-4-NullLock-oneExec`, at DISTINCT_ITEMS=1 — the
# difference only exists on contended work, and a null result at low contention is the predicted
# result, not a failed run.
#
# Two effects push opposite ways, which is the question:
#   - QUEUE POSITION. A retry now rejoins an unbounded FIFO at the TAIL, behind first attempts
#     admitted after it, so its real wait becomes backoff + queue depth. This can raise
#     `inventory_optimistic_exhausted_total` even though the lane is wider.
#   - DISPOSITION CONTENTION. OrderReservationSaga submits abandon()/release/fail-order to the same
#     executor, so a saturated pool now delays terminal dispositions behind retried work. Watch
#     `saga_command_failed_total{stage="abandon-rejected"}`, which exists for exactly that.
#
# WHAT TO READ. Both pools are instrumented with the same names ES-4-NullLock-mod uses, so panels
# built for that branch resolve here: `saga_pool_{active,queued,size}{pool="command"|"retry"}`.
# The two lanes mean different things on this branch than anywhere else:
#   pool="command"  active/queued is the whole story — `queued` is where a retry waits BEHIND newer
#                   first attempts, which is the cost this branch trades its wider lane for.
#   pool="retry"    `active` should sit at ~0; these threads only hand tasks over. Sustained
#                   activity there means work is executing on the timer, which it must not.
#                   `queued` is the in-flight retry count (a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor holds
#                   delayed tasks in its DelayedWorkQueue), NOT threads waiting for a slot.
# Note `size` is threads ALIVE, not the configured width: the pools fill lazily, so the series ramps
# and a mid-run value below 112 means only that many threads were ever needed at once.
#
# Threads are NAMED (`saga-command-N`, `retry-timer-N`) where every other ES branch leaves Executors'
# default `pool-N-thread-M`. That is what makes `jcmd <pid> Thread.print` answer "where does a retry
# execute?" by inspection, and logback's `[%thread]` pattern carries it into every line. Setting
# `logging.level.pl.szymanski.wiktor.config.ConcurrencyRetryScheduler=DEBUG` prints
# `[RETRY] executing on saga-command-N` per retry — the branch's headline property as one grep.
#
# It publishes one series no other branch has, `inventory_retry_handoff_rejected_total`. It should
# be 0: it counts retries that ran on the retry thread after the command pool refused the hand-off
# (shutdown only — the queue is unbounded), i.e. work that escaped the budgeted lane.
#
# `ES-4-NullLock-retry` is ES-4-NullLock with the RETRY POOL widened 4 -> 64 and nothing else — the
# command pool stays 64, and retry policy (5 attempts, 25/50/100/200 ms) is untouched. Four threads
# sound like a timer and are not one: Axon's `RetryingCallback` re-dispatches INLINE and
# `SimpleCommandBus` handles on the calling thread, so a retried command executes IN FULL on the
# retry pool. At 4 that is a 16x narrower waist than the first-attempt path, and on a lock-free
# branch — where conflicts are resolved by the event store's unique constraint plus this retry —
# most contended work goes through it. Run as a pair: `--only ES-4-NullLock,ES-4-NullLock-retry`,
# at the DISTINCT_ITEMS=1 hot-key point where the other NullLock variants are read.
#
# Needs `AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE=400`: a busy thread can hold two Axon connections at once, so peak
# demand is 2 x (64 + 64 + 60 + 3) = 382, where ES-4-NullLock now demands exactly the 350
# docker-compose passes. That is why the baseline fits it and this one does not; the API logs the
# arithmetic as [POOLS] at startup and warns when short. REPLICAS=1 only, unless PG_MAX_CONNECTIONS
# also rises. NOTE the command pool here is still 64 against the baseline's 82, so this is no longer
# a retry-width-only A/B either — see the ES-4-NullLock entry above.
#
# It shares `saga_pool_{active,queued,size}` with ES-4-NullLock-mod and has them on no other branch.
# Read `active`, not `queued`: a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor's queue also holds attempts still
# serving out their backoff.
#
# Distinguish it from `ES-4-NullLock-mod`, which widens BOTH pools (128 command / 32 retry) and so
# cannot say which of the two mattered. This branch is the controlled half of that question.
#
# `ES-1-NullLock` and `ES-2-NullLock` extend that A/B to the two simpler ES design points, so the
# lock's cost can be read apart from the cache's: ES-1 is uncached and unsnapshotted, ES-2 adds
# event-count snapshots, and neither has a cache to confound the comparison. Run them as pairs:
# `--only ES-1,ES-1-NullLock` and `--only ES-2,ES-2-NullLock`, at DISTINCT_ITEMS=1.
#
# THREE things differ from their base branches, not one. ES-1/ES-2 declare no repository bean at all
# and let Axon's implicit @Aggregate registration lock pessimistically, so the lock-free repository
# had to be introduced AND named on `@Aggregate(repository = ...)`; they also take ES-3/ES-4's
# gap-tracking tuning (`axon.eventstore.max-gap-offset` 500 instead of Axon's 10000), which is
# load-bearing once rolled-back appends are routine rather than rare; and since 2026-08-17 they
# carry the same LANE WIDTHS as ES-4-NullLock — command pool 64 -> 82, retry 23 -> 30, Tomcat
# capped at 99 where the base branches run Boot's default 200. Say so in any table: neither
# `--only ES-1,ES-1-NullLock` nor `--only ES-2,ES-2-NullLock` is a lock-only A/B any more, exactly
# as `--only ES-4,ES-4-NullLock` is not.
#
# All three *-NullLock variants now consume the 350 that docker-compose passes EXACTLY:
# 2 x (82 command + 30 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) = 350, one busy thread holding two Axon
# connections. Their 99 Tomcat threads sit OUTSIDE that sum by design — the accept command is
# dispatched with sendAndWait on the Tomcat thread and SimpleCommandBus handles it there, so peak
# demand is really 2 x (99 + 175) = 548 and the pool holds only because offered load, not the
# thread cap, bounds POSTs in flight. Watch
# `hikaricp_connections_timeout_total{pool="axon-jdbc-pool"}` on every run of the three: a starved
# command stalls 5s and then fails TERMINALLY, surfacing as latency and rejections, not as an error.
#
# There is no `ES-3-NullLock`. ES-3 caches with Axon's `WeakReferenceCache` and has no
# copy-on-write, so without a lock concurrent commands would mutate one shared aggregate root.
# Only ES-4's repository deep-copies per load, which is what makes ES-4-NullLock safe.
#
# `ES-4-NullLock-mod` is ES-4-NullLock with the command executors WIDENED and nothing else:
# axon.saga.command-pool-size 64 -> 128 and axon.saga.retry-pool-size 4 -> 32. Retry POLICY is
# byte-identical (maxRetries=5, initialDelayMs=25, 500ms cap), so a difference cannot be blamed on
# the backoff curve. The retry pool matters far more than its 4 threads suggest: Axon's
# RetryingCallback re-dispatches INLINE and SimpleCommandBus handles on the calling thread, so a
# retried command's aggregate load, reserveDelayMs sleep and append all run on that pool — which on
# a lock-free branch is where most contended work ends up.
#
# Run it as a pair — `--only ES-4-NullLock,ES-4-NullLock-mod` — but at DISTINCT_ITEMS >= 32, the
# OPPOSITE of the lock A/B above. NullLockFactory removes the JVM lock, not the event store's
# UNIQUE (aggregate_identifier, sequence_number), so one append per aggregate at a time still holds
# and the useful width is bounded by the item count; at DISTINCT_ITEMS=1 the extra threads buy
# conflicts and this variant should be WORSE than its baseline.
#
# Two operational requirements the suite cannot enforce, both from the widened pools:
#   - export AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE=500. A busy thread holds TWO Axon connections at once (its
#     command's transaction, plus the one DataSourceConnectionProvider opens per append), so peak
#     demand is 2 x (128 + 32 + 60 + 3) = 446. docker-compose passes AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE with a
#     default of 350 and that OVERRIDES the branch's application.yaml, so the branch cannot fix this
#     for itself. The API logs the arithmetic as `[POOLS]` at startup and warns when it is short.
#   - REPLICAS=1 only, unless PG_MAX_CONNECTIONS also rises: 2 x (50 + 500) > 600.
#
# `ES-4-NullLock-A` was RETIRED FROM THE REGISTRY on 2026-08-17 and is a branch only — LOCAL ONLY at
# that date, never pushed to origin. It is ES-4-NullLock with the order-saga moved from a
# TrackingEventProcessor to a PooledStreamingEventProcessor. The domain write path is byte-identical
# — NullLockFactory, copy-on-write repository, cache-fed snapshotter, retry policy — and
# total-segments stays 60, so token_entry is compatible in BOTH directions and switching between the
# pair needs no token reset.
#
# Two reasons it was pulled, and the second is the one to fix before re-adding a row:
#
#   1. `--only ES-4-NullLock,ES-4-NullLock-A` stopped being single-variable, varying processor type
#      and lane widths together. ES-4-NullLock has since moved again (command 82, retry 30, Tomcat
#      99), widening the gap.
#   2. THE DESCRIPTION BELOW IS AHEAD OF THE CODE. It says the envelope change moved the command
#      pool to 91, capped Tomcat at 45 and raised axon.jdbc.pool.size to 350. The branch as it
#      stands has COMMAND_POOL_SIZE 64, RETRY_POOL_SIZE 23, no `server.tomcat` block at all (so
#      Boot's default 200) and never reads HTTP_THREADS. The `12 http + 91 command` arithmetic
#      below was never implemented. Reconcile the two before benching it again — a run against
#      these notes would be attributed to lane widths the branch does not have.
#
# Everything below about TEP vs PSEP mechanics is still accurate and worth keeping; only the lane
# arithmetic is aspirational.
#
# TEP spends one thread PER SEGMENT, and that is structural rather than a sizing choice:
# forParallelProcessing(n) sets maxThreadCount = n and WorkerLauncher claims a segment only while
# availableThreads > 0, so fewer threads means permanently unclaimed segments and orders stranded in
# PENDING. Sixty segments cost sixty threads holding up to 120 of the 300-connection Axon pool while
# doing no aggregate work: the saga handler updates saga state and SUBMITS to sagaCommandExecutor.
# Each TEP worker also opens its OWN stream, and one that falls further behind than
# EmbeddedEventStore's 10 000-event shared cache takes a PRIVATE connection with an open ResultSet
# until it catches up — worst exactly during a saturation run.
#
# PSEP decouples the two: one Coordinator thread owns a SINGLE event stream and feeds per-segment
# WorkPackages onto a shared pool of `axon.saga.worker-threads` (default 64, SAGA_WORKER_THREADS).
#   ES-4-NullLock    2 x (64 + 23 + 60 + 3) = 300 connections — exactly the pool, no headroom
#   ES-4-NullLock-A  2 x (12 + 64 + 31 + 64 + 1 + 3) = 350 — exactly the pool
#
# The point of PSEP is NOT that the saga runs on fewer threads. It is that the saga width stops
# being pinned to the segment count and becomes a free variable — which is what let it be RAISED
# from 12 to 64 once measurement showed the saga lane, not the pool, was the constraint. Its
# threads come from capping Tomcat at 12. total-segments moves 60 -> 64 to match the worker width,
# so token_entry is no longer interchangeable with ES-4-NullLock: switching needs a token reset
# (reset.sh already truncates token_entry every run).
#
# Both of those sums omitted the ACCEPT lane until 2026-08-16, and that was the larger error.
# POST /inventory/orders calls sendAndWait, SimpleCommandBus handles on the CALLING thread, so a
# Tomcat thread runs the command handler itself and holds two axon-jdbc-pool connections for the
# whole request. Against Boot's default 200-thread connector that is up to 400 connections nobody
# budgeted, on top of a sum already sized to exactly fill the pool. An overrun does not fail
# cleanly: axonDataSource sets connectionTimeout=5000 and ConcurrencyRetryScheduler will not retry
# a SQLTransientConnectionException, so a starved command stalls 5s and fails TERMINALLY, reading
# as latency and a rejection rate. Suspect it in any pre-2026-08-16 ES saturation run —
# `hikaricp_connections_timeout_total{pool="axon-jdbc-pool"}` is the series that shows it.
#
# NO EXPORT NEEDED: docker-compose's AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE default is 350 as of 2026-08-16, which is
# exactly this branch's demand. The variable still OVERRIDES the branch's application.yaml whatever
# it is set to, so a run that exports a smaller value silently wins; the API logs what actually took
# effect as `[POOLS]` at startup and warns when the pool is short.
#
# That default is global to the ES family, which is the cost of it: ES-1/ES-2/ES-3/ES-4 and the base
# *-NullLock variants ask for 300 in their own yaml and now get 350 — 50 connections of headroom
# they did not have. Their documented budgets still fit, so this is not a correctness change, but
# runs made before and after 2026-08-16 are not config-identical and should not share a table.
# -mod (500) and -retry still need their own larger exports; the default does not cover them.
#
# It publishes `saga_pool_{active,queued,size}` for `pool="saga-worker"`, `pool="command"` and
# `pool="retry"`, named to match -mod's gauges so one panel covers both. `saga_pool_queued` at 0
# while order throughput is flat means that pool was never the cap; `saga_pool_active{pool="retry"}`
# pinned at its size means the run measured retry width rather than the persistence model.
#
# It is a change to the ES INFRASTRUCTURE rather than to the domain, so if it wins it has to land on
# all seven ES variants before any cross-variant table is rebuilt — otherwise ES-vs-ES numbers mix
# two processor architectures.
#
# One harness caveat, and it belongs here because the suite cannot see it: evaluate.py's
# `saga_command_failed_single_node` VALIDITY check assumes a JVM-local lock makes a 23505
# impossible at REPLICAS=1. That is false for every *-NullLock variant by construction, so a
# single-node run of one reports INVALID whenever any command exhausts its retries. Read the rest
# of the check list; the run is sound if that is its only failure.
#
# Order matters: run-suite.sh runs them top to bottom. Families are grouped so a single
# suite pass crosses the TO/ES boundary once rather than once per variant; each crossing
# costs a full stack teardown because the two families use different compose service names.

TO-1  TO-1  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2  TO-2  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2-opt  TO-2-opt  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2-newT  TO-2-newT  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-3  TO-3  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-4  TO-4  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-1  ES-1  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-1-NullLock  ES-1-NullLock  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-2  ES-2  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-2-NullLock  ES-2-NullLock  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-3  ES-3  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-4  ES-4  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-4-NullLock  ES-4-NullLock  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-4-NullLock-oneExec  ES-4-NullLock-oneExec  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
